name  = mat4xvec4_f32
shape = the mat4xvec4 kernel at single precision, 20,000 vertices, 5000 passes
args  = 20000
cflags = -ffp-contract=off
claim = FOUR LANES INSTEAD OF TWO, on the kernel where two already paid.
        mat4xvec4 showed that explicit floating-point lanes beat both the
        scalar Mere row and C when the lanes are on the axis the reduction is
        not on. f64x2 is only two lanes wide, which is the whole width a
        128-bit vector of doubles has; this row is the same kernel with the
        lanes single precision, so a vec4 fits in ONE vector and a vertex costs
        four multiplies, three adds and one accumulate -- eight vector
        operations against the sixteen the f64 row needs. Eight against sixteen
        is the ceiling; the number says how much of it arrives.
        ---
        THIS ROW'S ANSWER IS NOT COMPARABLE WITH mat4xvec4'S. They are
        different computations -- single against double precision -- not two
        spellings of one, and that is why this is a separate directory rather
        than a bench_*.mere beside it: run.py's same-answer rule is per
        directory, and it would be wrong here and right there. What IS
        comparable is the WALL CLOCK, because the two kernels have the same
        shape, the same inputs, the same pass count and the same accumulation
        order; the README carries the pair.
        ---
        Within this directory the same-answer rule applies as usual, and here
        it means C's `float` and Rust's `f32` doing the arithmetic in the same
        order as Mere's lanes, including the final fold: f32x4_reduce_add is
        specified left to right at lane precision, and both reference rows fold
        their four accumulators the same way.
        ---
        The f32 accumulator does NOT saturate, which was measured rather than
        assumed -- the checksum still changes between 4999 and 5000 passes, so
        every pass reaches the answer and the same-answer check is live. Had it
        saturated, a wrong implementation could have agreed with a right one by
        both running out of mantissa, which is the shape of a check that passes
        for the wrong reason.
        ---
        Same floating-point discipline as the rest of the suite. Unfused: Mere
        emits `#pragma STDC FP_CONTRACT OFF`, the C row carries
        -ffp-contract=off above, Rust does not contract without mul_add.
        Non-dyadic inputs (divided by 1000) so the arithmetic rounds. The
        answer is the IEEE-754 pattern of the widened accumulator, because a
        formatted double hides the low bits.
        ---
        No Go row: Go has no 128-bit vector type a program can write, so the
        comparison it would make -- scalar float32 against explicit lanes -- is
        the one the C and Rust rows already make. No Node, Ruby or Python row,
        for the reason mat4xvec4 gives: none of the three has an unboxed
        single-precision array with 28 operations per element.

# 1,048,952 B at v0.1.444 -- the same two Vecs as mat4xvec4, because the inputs
# are still doubles: the narrowing happens at the lane, not in the buffer. That
# a single-precision kernel does not halve its memory here is a fact about the
# input representation and is the first thing a `bytes`-backed vertex buffer
# would change (Q-123).
alloc_min = 786432
alloc_max = 1310720
